Cues describe frame-owned events for Rust consumers; their emit strings are not commands executed by the animation engine. [[events]]
on = { frame_enter = 4 }
emit = "Attack" [[events]]
on = { frame_exit = 6 }
emit = "Recover" [[events]]
on = { anim_finished = true }
emit = "Finished" on.bookmark selects frame entry at a named bookmark. If both frame_enter and bookmark are supplied, the explicit frame takes precedence. Entry, exit, and completion selectors can coexist in one event definition; prefer one trigger per entry for clarity. Completion cues run on completion, not interruption or an indefinitely held clip. Playback publishes ordered start, frame-entry, frame-exit, cue, loop, seek, completion, and interruption events tagged with PlaybackId. AnimationSet::Signals publishes the current tick's events into each animation entity's PlaybackState component after script execution. Consumers must match the playback execution they own. A held frame emits no repeated entry cue, while a real loop or seek to another frame creates new entries. Action cancellation and cleanup remain in game code.

