The music controller (game/src/music.rs) owns the soundtrack independently of animation scripts. Entering gameplay requests track main on the next beat. Track metadata (assets/audio/music/tracks.music.toml) maps this to the existing audio.game.Music / Music3 binding. The editable metadata includes source sample rate, first downbeat, loop start/end, gain, and beats per bar. The default starts at source frame zero, loops [0, 15_360_000) at 48 kHz (480 beats), and uses gain 0.04. The additional 1,920 silent frames in the source are outside the loop; the file is unchanged. A nonzero first downbeat starts playback at that source offset, skipping an intro. Game systems use MessageWriter<MusicCommand> to submit these commands (commands below is that message writer): commands.write(MusicCommand::Play { track: "main".into(), boundary: MusicBoundary::NextBeat,
});
commands.write(MusicCommand::Transition { track: "another_track".into(), boundary: MusicBoundary::NextBar, fade_bars: 1,
});
commands.write(MusicCommand::SetVolume(0.8));
commands.write(MusicCommand::Stop { boundary: MusicBoundary::NextBeat }); NextBeat and NextBar select strictly later boundaries. Immediate selects the current admitted simulation target, which can still be ahead of audible playback. Beat/bar boundaries belong to the global grid. Transitions crossfade for one bar by default; fade_bars is configurable. A track that is not prepared leaves the current track playing and chooses the next eligible boundary once ready. Music volume changes the music bus, including both sides of a crossfade, without disturbing voice envelopes. The default Play also fades in over one bar. Stop uses a short 10 ms fade at its selected boundary. Reconnection schedules a restart on the next beat and seeks to the track's elapsed transport phase; recovery need not be acoustically gapless.

