ScriptPlayer<T> owns a playback request and its runtime. T is Script for common scripts or SpriteAnimation for animations. Systems can set slots, pause, stop, or request another asset. use bevy::prelude::*;
use theseeker_engine::assets::script::Script;
use theseeker_engine::script::ScriptPlayer; fn mark_ready(mut players: Query<&mut ScriptPlayer<Script>>) { for mut player in &mut players { if player.current_key() == Some("script.example.Ambience") { player.set_slot("Ready", true); } }
} Slots belong to the particular player/runtime; they are not global flags. A [config] table is parsed into the asset's config field, a map of DynamicConfigValue values. Rust code that needs those values reads them from the loaded asset in Assets<Script> and matches the Int, Float, or String variant; 7 and 7.0 are different variants. play_key queues a request. ScriptSet::Init commits requests before ScriptSet::Run advances playback in GameTickUpdate. Whether a request starts in the current or a later tick depends on when it was submitted and whether its asset is ready. Until a replacement commits, ordinary slot/config access still addresses the old runtime. Slots set before the first runtime exists are retained for initialization. Actor presentation also has execution-scoped overrides; use that ownership API for actor behavior. A completed common script stops running, but its entity remains until explicitly despawned. A slot-only script does not remain active indefinitely just because it declares slot triggers. See the reference's lifetime rules before authoring persistent listeners.
